  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jeffrey Springs gave them everything a starter can give, and it didn't matter.",
    "what_this_means": "The bullpen collapsed in the eighth. Luis Medina threw zero outs and gave up two runs without recording a single strikeout. Jos\u00e9 Suarez followed and allowed three more before getting one out. By then, San Francisco had eight runs and the game was over. Carlos Cortes hit 2-for-4 with an RBI. The only run they scored. But one run is not a game in September baseball. Five hits and six strikeouts is not an offensive night. This is what happens when the starter does his job and the offense doesn't do theirs. The pitching was fine. The bats were silent. That's the actual split.",
    "what_to_watch": "They are now 23-23, tied in the division but underwater in every way that matters. The fragility isn't new, but it's showing up in the same place. The late innings, the leverage moments, the spots where one good AB changes everything. Springs held them in the game. The lineup couldn't hold the game back. When the bullpen broke in the eighth, nobody was close enough to the plate to care.",
